  "created_at": "2026-09-18T17:14:56.816Z",
  "assessment": {
    "status": "contradicted",
    "confidence": 0.85,
    "reasoning": "The proposition is the danger thesis that anchored public concern about the Large Hadron Collider before its 2008 start, and the examined evidence weighs against it on each of its legs. The claim is an extreme conditional compound: it requires that the LHC produce black holes at all, which presupposes speculative physics in which [[claim:e382b679-7f22-451c-a551-864fc0c798b5|large extra dimensions lower the Planck scale to the TeV range]], a scenario collider and astrophysical constraints have increasingly disfavored and dedicated searches have never confirmed; it requires stability against evaporation, contrary to the strong theoretical expectation that [[claim:fcab83dc-9428-40e2-86dc-457774afaafa|any collider-produced black hole evaporates essentially instantly via Hawking radiation]]; and it requires that [[claim:6a7966e4-5704-4295-adbf-837de96f26cd|a stable trapped black hole accrete matter fast enough to grow and destroy the planet]], the step on which the direct evidence turns.\n\nThe peer-reviewed analysis of Giddings and Mangano (https://arxiv.org/abs/0806.3381, Physical Review D 78, 035009 (2008)) grants production and stability for the sake of argument and finds no surviving danger: scenarios in which a trapped black hole could grow dangerously fast are excluded because cosmic rays striking far denser white dwarfs and neutron stars would have produced the same black holes and destroyed those long-lived, well-observed bodies long ago. The observational premise is solid: [[claim:11887800-2f7b-4ac5-99c2-4dc5bb2f7f7a|cosmic rays above LHC energies have struck Earth, the Moon, and dense stars for billions of years]]. Independent work reaches the same verdict, including the CERN-affiliated review by Ellis, Giudice, Mangano, Tkachev, and Wiedemann (https://arxiv.org/abs/0806.3414) and the Casadio, Fabi, and Harms analysis in the warped brane-world framework (https://arxiv.org/abs/0901.2948). A decade and a half of LHC operation, including dedicated CMS and ATLAS searches with no microscopic black hole events, weighs further against production at any reachable scale.\n\nThe one credible contrary voice is Plaga's preprint (https://arxiv.org/abs/0808.1415), which constructs a metastable scenario in which black holes accrete at the Eddington limit and evade the astrophysical exclusions; it concedes that the mainstream analyses are technically correct under their assumptions and attributes the different verdicts to different initial assumptions. Giddings and Mangano's published reply (https://arxiv.org/abs/0808.4087) argues the scenario is internally inconsistent, the claimed black hole radius exceeding the Schwarzschild radius for the relevant mass range by many orders of magnitude, and that the white dwarf and neutron star bounds would apply regardless. The literature has not taken the scenario up. For the claim to revive, a derivation would need to show dangerous accretion rates for a stable trapped black hole in a parameter range that survives the compact-star constraints; no published argument presently does."
  }
